---
import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { business } from '../../data/business';
---

<ServiceLayout
  title="Splashback Installation Wellington | Kitchen & Bathroom | ROH Tiling"
  description={`Tiled splashback installation across Wellington. Subway, fluted, mosaic and large-format tile, cut neatly around power points. Free quotes — call ${business.phone}.`}
  canonical="/services/splashback-installation"
  serviceName="Splashback Installation"
  serviceType="Splashback Installation"
  serviceDescription="Kitchen and bathroom splashback installation in tile, subway, fluted and mosaic finishes, cut around power points and window reveals, across the Wellington region."
>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in-up" style="max-width: 800px;">
        <p class="lead">
          A splashback is a small job that everybody looks at. It sits at eye level behind the bench, it is lit from underneath by the cabinet lights, and it is the one piece of tiling in the kitchen that guests stand next to while they talk to you. That is why the cuts matter more here than almost anywhere else in the house.
        </p>
        <p>
          I install tiled splashbacks in kitchens, bathrooms, laundries and butler's pantries across {business.areaServed.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and the {business.areaServed.at(-1)}. Most are a half-day to a day on site, and most can be done without pulling the kitchen apart.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What we install -->
  <section class="section section--light">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Splashbacks We Install</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">Whatever the tile, the job is the same: get it flat, get it level, and get the cuts right.</p>

      <div class="services-grid fade-in-up">
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Subway Tile</h3>
          <p>The default for a reason. Laid in a running bond, stack bond or herringbone, in gloss or matt. The look changes completely depending on the grout colour you pick, so it is worth deciding that before the tiles go on rather than after.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Fluted and Ribbed Tile</h3>
          <p>Vertical ribbed tile throws a shadow line that flat tile cannot. It needs the verticals kept dead plumb across the whole run and the ribs lined up through the corners, or the eye picks it up immediately.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Mosaic and Penny Round</h3>
          <p>Sheet-mounted mosaics fill an awkward space well and suit a narrow strip between the bench and the overhead cupboards. The join between sheets has to be spaced the same as the tiles themselves or it shows as a seam.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Large-Format and Slab Look</h3>
          <p>One or two big pieces instead of a grid, so there is almost no grout to keep clean behind the cooktop. Needs a flat wall underneath and careful handling, but it is the easiest splashback there is to wipe down.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Bathroom and Vanity Splashbacks</h3>
          <p>A tiled strip behind the basin, or a full wall run to the mirror. Usually done in the same tile as the rest of the bathroom so it reads as one surface rather than an add-on.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <h3>Laundry and Butler's Pantry</h3>
          <p>The rooms that take the most abuse and get the least thought. A tiled splashback behind the tub or the second sink stops water getting into the wall lining and is easier to clean than paint.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The detail that matters -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Where Splashbacks Go Wrong</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">The parts of the job that separate a splashback you stop noticing from one you notice every day.</p>

      <div class="process-timeline fade-in-up">
        <div class="process-step">
          <h3>Cuts around power points</h3>
          <p>Sockets and switches sit in the middle of the run, and a badly cut tile around one is the first thing you see. The cut should sit under the faceplate with an even margin, not be covered by it at one corner and gaping at the other. Where the layout allows, I set out so a socket lands on a grout line rather than mid-tile.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <h3>Setting out from the bench, not the ceiling</h3>
          <p>Benchtops are level; walls and overhead cupboards frequently are not. The tile course is set off the bench so the bottom row reads straight against the thing your eye compares it to, and any discrepancy is absorbed up at the cupboards where nobody looks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <h3>Window reveals and returns</h3>
          <p>Where a splashback runs into a window over the sink, the tile has to return into the reveal or stop cleanly against the frame. Mitred external corners look better than a bullnose trim, but they need the tile cut properly rather than ground back until it fits.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <h3>Silicone, not grout, at the movement joints</h3>
          <p>The junction where the tile meets the benchtop moves as the house does. Grout there will crack within a year. It gets a colour-matched silicone bead instead, and so does every internal corner in the run.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <h3>Behind the cooktop</h3>
          <p>Gas cooktops need the right clearance to combustible surfaces, and the tile behind them takes heat and grease. Substrate and adhesive get chosen with that in mind rather than defaulting to whatever is in the van.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Practicalities -->
  <section class="section section--light">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="fade-in-up" style="max-width: 800px;">
        <h2 class="section-title section-title--left">What to Expect</h2>
        <p>
          Most kitchen splashbacks are a one to two day job: a day to set out and tile, then back to grout and silicone once the adhesive has gone off. The bench gets covered and the cabinets get protected before anything starts, and the site is swept and wiped down before I leave.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the kitchen is new, the splashback goes on after the benchtop is installed and before the appliances go in. If it is an existing kitchen, old tile or old splashback comes off first and the wall gets made good, which is worth allowing for in the timing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tiles are usually supplied by you or picked up on your behalf. Getting the quantity right matters, so allow around ten percent over the measured area for cuts and breakages, more if the tile is being laid in a herringbone or diagonal pattern.
        </p>
        <p>
          Working on a full kitchen? Splashbacks pair naturally with <a href="/services/kitchen-tiling">kitchen floor tiling</a>, and if the job is part of a bathroom it is usually done alongside the <a href="/services/bathroom-tiling">bathroom tiling</a> and <a href="/services/waterproofing">waterproofing</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</ServiceLayout>
